# ============================================================================
# Anestheo — root cutover .htaccess
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT ACTIVE.
#
# It is deliberately named "htaccess-root-cutover" and kept under deploy/ so
# that uploading the repository cannot switch redirects on by accident. To
# activate it, copy it to public_html/.htaccess as a separate, deliberate step
# in the cutover sequence — see ROOT_CUTOVER.md.
#
# Hostinger shared hosting runs LiteSpeed, which reads .htaccess and implements
# mod_rewrite, mod_alias and ErrorDocument with Apache semantics. Everything
# used here is within that supported subset. Nothing here needs shell access,
# and nothing here requires a server restart: LiteSpeed re-reads .htaccess per
# request, so a mistake is reverted in the time it takes to re-upload the file.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. The blocks below run top to bottom and the first [L] wins:
#
#   1. DirectoryIndex          — "/" serves index.html
#   2. Videos exception        — /videos.html serves the preserved V1 page
#   3. Legacy V1 URLs          — old V1 pages 301 to their V2 equivalents
#   4. Legacy /v2 URLs         — /v2/anything 301 to /anything
#   5. ErrorDocument           — 404.html
#   6. Source file denial      — .sql, .md, .py, dotfiles
#
# The videos rule is FIRST among the rewrites on purpose: it is the one
# deliberate exception to "the root is the V2 application", and putting it
# first means no later rule can accidentally capture it.
#
# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
#   * No HTTPS or www canonicalisation. Hostinger already terminates both at
#     the edge; adding rules here risks a redirect loop with that layer. If you
#     want them, verify what the panel is already doing FIRST.
#   * No caching headers. They are drafted at the foot of this file, commented
#     out, and are not part of the cutover.
# ============================================================================

# ── 1. Directory index ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A request for "/" is served from index.html. This is what makes the preferred
# homepage URL anestheo.com/ rather than anestheo.com/index.html. Both work;
# only one is advertised.
DirectoryIndex index.html


<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On

  # ── 2. VIDEOS — the one deliberate V1 exception ───────────────────────────
  #
  # The V1 videos page is the preferred one and stays live at /videos.html
  # until the dedicated port task replaces it. It cannot simply sit at the
  # document root: it is a V1 page, and V1 pages load V1 auth.js / supabase.js
  # / style.css — all of which are either replaced by V2 files at the root or
  # gone. A V1 page reading V2 JavaScript is the hybrid we are avoiding.
  #
  # So the V1 page and EVERY file it depends on live together, isolated, in
  # public_html/videos-v1/. Nothing in that folder can reach a V2 file, and
  # nothing V2 can reach into it.
  #
  # These are INTERNAL rewrites, not redirects. There is no [R], so the browser
  # never sees videos-v1/ and the visible URL stays /videos.html. That matters:
  # the URL is already indexed and already linked from the V2 navbar.
  #
  # ONE CONSEQUENCE YOU MUST HANDLE, and it is not optional:
  # because the visible URL stays /videos.html, the browser resolves the page's
  # RELATIVE references against "/" — not against /videos-v1/. A page asking for
  # style.css would fetch /style.css and get a 404 (or, worse, a leftover V1
  # file). This was reproduced: the preserved page loaded with two 404s until
  # fixed.
  #
  # The fix is one line, added to the preserved copy as the FIRST tag in <head>:
  #
  #     <base href="/videos-v1/">
  #
  # With it, every relative reference resolves inside the isolated folder and
  # nothing escapes to the root. Any ROOT-ABSOLUTE reference in the V1 page
  # (src="/auth.js") ignores <base> and must be changed to relative by hand —
  # the dependency capture step in ROOT_CUTOVER.md is what finds those.
  #
  # No loop: the substitution "videos-v1/index.html" does not match ^videos\.html$
  # or ^videos-v1\.html$, so neither rule can re-fire.
  #
  # WHEN THE PORT TASK LANDS: delete this whole block. /videos.html then falls
  # through to the V2 file already sitting at the root, with no other change.
  RewriteRule ^videos\.html$     videos-v1/index.html  [L]
  RewriteRule ^videos-v1\.html$  videos-v1/index.html  [L]


  # ── 3. Legacy V1 URLs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  #
  # These V1 pages are archived out of the live root at cutover, so without
  # these rules every one of them would 404 for anyone holding a bookmark or
  # arriving from a search result. Each destination is the V2 page that does
  # the same job; the mapping table and its reasoning are in ROOT_CUTOVER.md.
  #
  # 301 (permanent) because these moves are permanent and we want search
  # engines to transfer the old pages' standing to the new ones.
  #
  # No loop: every destination is a real V2 file at the root, and no
  # destination appears on the left-hand side of any rule above or below.

  # Authentication — V2 has no standalone login page. Sign-in and registration
  # are a modal in navbar.js, reachable from the homepage. This is the closest
  # honest destination, not an exact equivalent. See ROOT_CUTOVER.md.
  RewriteRule ^auth\.html$        /index.html            [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^register\.html$    /index.html            [R=301,L]

  # Role workspaces
  RewriteRule ^doctor\.html$      /dashboard.html        [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^patient\.html$     /patient-dashboard.html [R=301,L]

  # Clinical tools and reference
  RewriteRule ^calculator\.html$  /engine.html           [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^calculators\.html$ /engine.html           [R=301,L]
  RewriteRule ^clinical\.html$    /references.html       [R=301,L]

  # Legal. V1 had one combined page; V2 splits it five ways. /terms.html is the
  # entry point and links to the rest. FLAGGED AS AMBIGUOUS in ROOT_CUTOVER.md.
  RewriteRule ^legal\.html$       /terms.html            [R=301,L]

  # The V1 questionnaire. Note the spaces in the original filename — they
  # arrive percent-encoded, so the pattern matches the decoded form and the
  # encoded one. FLAGGED AS AMBIGUOUS in ROOT_CUTOVER.md.
  RewriteRule ^Pre-operative(%20|\s)anesthesia(%20|\s)questionnaire\.html$ /questionnaire.html [R=301,L,NC]

  # V1 fragment include — never a page a human should land on.
  RewriteRule ^_nav\.html$        /                      [R=301,L]

  # NOT REDIRECTED, deliberately: about.html, admin.html, ask.html,
  # index.html, patients.html. V2 has a file of the same name at the root, so
  # these URLs keep working by themselves and a rule would be a no-op at best
  # and a loop at worst.
  #
  # NOT REDIRECTED, needs a decision: contact.html. There is no V2 equivalent.
  # It is marked ARCHIVE/REVIEW in ROOT_CUTOVER.md and will 404 (with the 404
  # page) until someone decides. Add a rule here once that is settled.


  # ── 4. Legacy /v2 URLs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  # 4a. The bare prefix, with or without a trailing slash, is the homepage.
  RewriteRule ^v2/?$ / [R=301,L]

  # 4b. Everything below the prefix keeps its path.
  #     /v2/engine.html          -> /engine.html
  #     /v2/q.html?t=ABC123      -> /q.html?t=ABC123
  #     /v2/styles.css?v=2026…   -> /styles.css?v=2026…
  #
  #     The query string is preserved automatically: a substitution that
  #     contains no "?" leaves the original query untouched. QSA is NOT used
  #     here — it is for merging a query the rule itself introduces, and adding
  #     it would risk duplicating parameters.
  #
  #     WHY THIS CANNOT LOOP: the substitution "/$1" never begins with "v2/",
  #     because the pattern consumed that prefix. The redirected request is a
  #     fresh external request for /engine.html, which does not match ^v2/ and
  #     therefore passes straight through. There is exactly one hop.
  #
  #     A token in a query string survives this untouched, which is the whole
  #     point: every questionnaire link already sent by email or WhatsApp still
  #     opens the right patient's questionnaire.
  #
  #     Note /v2/videos.html: it 301s to /videos.html, which then internally
  #     rewrites to the preserved V1 page. One redirect, then a rewrite the
  #     browser never sees.
  RewriteRule ^v2/(.*)$ /$1 [R=301,L]
</IfModule>


# ── 5. Not found ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The path is root-absolute, so it resolves the same way for a missing page at
# any depth. 404.html itself loads only root-absolute assets, so it renders
# correctly whether the bad URL was /nope.html or /a/b/c/nope.html.
ErrorDocument 404 /404.html


# ── 6. Do not serve source or operational files ─────────────────────────────
# The repository contains .sql migrations and .md documents that have no reason
# to be web-readable. This is defence in depth for the class of problem already
# identified at the root: V1 left supabase_migration_final.sql publicly
# downloadable. Schema disclosure is not a breach on its own — RLS is the
# boundary — but it hands an attacker the map for free.
<FilesMatch "\.(sql|md|py|log|bak|old|orig|ini|sh|yml|yaml)$">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Dotfiles and version-control leftovers.
<FilesMatch "^\.">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>
RedirectMatch 404 /\.git


